Corosync on FenrirOS
====================

The package installs corosync inert: no /etc/corosync/corosync.conf, no
/etc/corosync/authkey, and its dinit services are not enabled.

Built-in features: dbus, monitoring (libstatgrab), watchdog, augeas lens,
snmp, xmlconf, vqsim, nozzle. Encryption is enforced (corosync 3.2's
default): crypto_cipher/crypto_hash "none" and the legacy udp/udpu
transports are not compiled in, so every cluster link is knet with
encryption.

On a Proxmox VE node, pve-cluster (pvecm create / pvecm add) writes the
configuration and the key; nothing below is needed by hand.

Standalone setup:

1. Configuration: start from the example
     cp /usr/share/doc/corosync/corosync.conf.example /etc/corosync/corosync.conf
   and set totem.cluster_name, the nodelist (one ring0_addr per node), and
   crypto_cipher/crypto_hash (e.g. aes256 / sha256).
2. Key, the same file on every node (mode 0400, owner root):
     corosync-keygen
   then copy /etc/corosync/authkey to the other nodes.
3. Start it, and enable it at boot if wanted:
     dinitctl start corosync
     dinitctl enable corosync
   corosync-notifyd (D-Bus signals; SNMP traps with -s -m <manager>, see
   /etc/dinit.d/corosync-notifyd) is a separate service:
     dinitctl start corosync-notifyd
4. Check: corosync-cfgtool -s, corosync-quorumtool -s.

Logs go to syslog and, if configured, /var/log/cluster/. A logrotate
snippet is in /etc/logrotate.d/corosync (used only when logrotate is
installed).
